ful in the  vast majority of people but we do know  that some people respond at very the  respond very nicely in some cases with  complete shrinkage of the tumors so in  those particular patients those  individuals have tend to have very high  levels of already existing immune  responses now in ovarian cancer we see  very limited activity in these immune  checkpoint mob blockade molecules in  ovarian cancer much less than we see in  malignant melanoma or non-small-cell  lung cancer so for example in this  particular study there was two complete  responses out of all of the several  patients that were treated in this  clinical trial and what we have have and  others have observed is that the  patients that don't respond don't have  t-cells so what happens or what would  what would happen if we combined a  immune checkpoint blockade molecules  such as anti PD 1 or anti PDL one with  vaccines to boost those immune responses  and this is exactly what we did in this  mouse model of breast cancer in which we  knives mice with a vaccine and then we  gave them anti pd-1 and I showed you on  the previous slide that these animals  can actually undergo regression of the  tumors but it grows out what we observed  is that we combine vaccine with immune  checkpoint blockade we have 75 percent  complete regression rate in these  animals and some ultimately redevelop  cancer and don't survive as a result but  this is extending the life of these mice  from on the order of 25 or 30 days with  breast cancer to over a hundred and  fifty days or a half-year so we think  that this is an approach that we could  potentially use in the clinic to treat  patients that have active malignancy so  this is different than the setting of  preventing recurrence in individuals but  rather giving them a vaccine and  overriding the regulatory mechanisms in  the tumor micro-environment with immune  checkpoint blockade leading to improve  survival and this is borne out in their  new responses as well so 