 of opportunity that we  can continue to boost the immune  response shown in the green line there  and we can do that with vaccines so more  needs to be done in this disease-free  period let's not wait and see if the  cancer comes back  let's give individuals with vaccine  booster immune response maintain that  immunity in order to prevent disease  from coming back so we're learning that  new ways to develop these vaccines as I  already talked about particularly  identifying the aberrantly Express  proteins  and hopefully formulating those into  effective approaches that prevent  recurrence we're also learning ways that  the immune system is blocking the  t-cells and b-cells in the micro  environment so we're working on ways of  what we call depolarizing immune  responses making them a multitude of  different flavors that could potentially  work in concert together to eradicate  the cancer we're also thinking about  combining with antibody therapy antibody  therapy also induces in the in responses  can we combine for example Herceptin  plus vaccine in patients that are at  high risk for redevelopment of her2 new  breast cancer or ovarian cancer or  similar cancers by combining vaccine  with monoclonal antibodies there are  also some potential opportunities in the  future for employing vaccines for the  treatment of individuals with bulky  malignancy but that's not going to be  with vaccines alone we think that's  going to be with the Muench at point  blockade immune checkpoint blockade  doesn't work in the vast majority of  individuals because they lack that  immune response we're going to give them  that immune response by him immunizing  them and treating them with an immune  checkpoint blockade clinical trials are  underway to test how do we do that in  patients with actively growing disease  one of the things that we need to do  though is continue to study individuals  for a new escape so some of these  approaches they may seem quite effective  but the tumors will grow out and so some  of the strategies that we're looking at  as we move forward in cancer vaccine  development is trying to understand what  new mechanisms the immune or the tumors  turn on in order to evade the immune  response I didn't talk about that today  but there's a number of strategies that  are being developed so all in all our  approach has been to use vaccines  appropriately either in disease  prevention or in combination  with the immune checkpoint blockade or  other types of antibody approaches we've  been very well funded by the Department  of Defense for several tens of millions  of dollars the National Institutes of  Health particularly the National Cancer  Institute and we've got a great team at  Mayo I'm not going to go into all of  these names but it really takes a good  collaborative team throughout the Mayo  Clinic Enterprise as well as individuals  outside of Mayo that have come together  to support the development of approaches  that we think are going to be in fact  effective at reducing the risk of death  from breast cancer and ovarian cancer  and I thank you very much for your  attention today  thank you dr. cannerts in for that  informative presentation we will now  start the Q&amp;A portion of the webinar and  we'll address some of the most commonly  asked questions by our viewers if you  have any questions you'd like to ask  please do so now just click on the ask a  question drop-down box located on the  far left of your presentation window  type your question into the box it  appears on your screen and click the  send button our speaker will follow up  with your questions via email let's get  started our first question is how are  cancer vaccines different than ordinary  infectious disease vaccines right that's  that's a really great question so just  to put it very simply they are different  in in some ways but the same in other  ways so vaccines are designed to boost  these T cells and these B cells in the  body those are very unique so when we  use a polio vaccine or an influenza  vaccine or measles vaccine or a tetanus  those are targeting something or  proteins that come from the infectious  agent so and they block viral entry and  they cause disease  if  if there's nothing there to stop them so  we're targeting with infectious disease  vaccines were targeting microbes  bacteria or viruses with infections or  excuse me with cancer vaccines in  contrast we don't have the same  opportunities for targeting microbes and  so we need to go into the cancers and  figure out what's different in the  cancers so we can identify proteins  which are not derived from microbes that  we can stimulate immune responses  against and so there while the concept  of vaccination to stimulate those  t-cells to stimulate those b-cells is  much the same the targets that we are  choosing for our vaccine are different  which chart were typically targeting  proteins that are present normally in  the body but are deranged in some way by  the cancer that the immune system can  see whereas with infectious disease  vaccines just to reiterate we're  actually targeting proteins that are  produced by the bacteria or the  infectious agent but are not normally  part of the body  thank you very interesting next one is  in what clinical cancer setting are  vaccines most likely to be effective  when used alone yeah so as I had  mentioned the the use of vaccines has  never been to treat disease we think  about influenza every year and many of  us get the flu and many of us get other  types of illnesses that can be prevented  with vaccines but not once if you have  the flu does the doctor offer you a flu  vaccine and the reason is is because by  that time it's too late and the flu has  already started to block the immune  system and in order to run its course  now with flu it runs its course for only  a  period of time it doesn't want to kill  its host and so it's adaptive ways to  you know make most of us sick it does  kill certain individuals particularly  the old that don't have immune systems  but the you know the the strategy is  that you you boost the immune systems so  that and the patient has immunity prior  to getting the disease and so in the in  cancer it's after we've eliminated the  disease with surgery with chemotherapy  then we boost the immune response and  when the tumors start to come back your  immune system remains heightened just  like it does after you get a flu vaccine  such that it can respond very rapidly  and overcome all of these immune  suppressive mechanisms and so the  clinical setting is to prevent disease  not to treat disease thank you for that  looks like we have time for one more  question if the immune system naturally  sees cancer why do they continue to grow  yeah so there's a number of different  strategies that they continue to grow  despite the fact that the immune system  they and and I briefly alluded to this  in the presentation that they develop  all these immune regulatory networks  that suppress the immune response so  your immune system is one of the most  powerful organs so to speak in your body  it can attack specific tissues very  specifically and over a very short  period of time and completely destroy  that tissue so somebody that has had a  transplant if you don't suppress their  immune system they can completely  eliminate that transplanted lung or that  transplanted heart or whatever tissue is  transplanted and they can it can happen  against cancer too but the reason that  cancers do grow out is because there are  a wide variety of immune suppressant  molecules normal  found in the immune system it's very  tightly controlled and different tissues  in your body use these mechanisms to  prevent the immune system from attacking  it now the thing about cancer is it uses  all of these mechanisms to block the  immune response and so this is very  critical because there's a number of  different immune suppressive mechanisms  so when we try 